00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
the pandemic has seen hospitals  everywhere filling up forcing many  people to postpone other medical  procedures  but one procedure in particular has been  booming  cosmetic surgery  with many people in lockdown unable to  leave their homes why are they going  under the knife to improve their  appearance  welcome to the covert 19 special i'm  rebecca ritters well it may seem  counter-intuitive but it's true in many  parts of the world the rate of plastic  surgery as well as other non-surgical  aesthetic treatments such as botox has  been soaring during the pandemic and  lockdown measures limited people's  contact with each other it seems many  have become more self-conscious about  the times they are being seen  tanya chang has undergone buttocks  treatment on her face  she did it during taiwan's lockdown when  she wouldn't come into contact with  other people for her it was a rare  opportunity  i'm in sales and i have to face clients  every day at work  it would be very inconvenient to have  bruises and swelling on my face after a  treatment  now that everyone wears a mask i don't  need to worry about that  so i am taking advantage of the pandemic  but that's just one of the reasons for  what has been a big boom in cosmetic  procedures all over the world  dermatologists in the us reported a  nearly 60 percent rise in patients  seeking aesthetic treatment since the  pandemic began  taking part in video conferences has  become routine for many people  and they have become conscious of  wrinkles and blemishes  ultimately people also want to look good  on screen  for more i'm joined by tony piku she's a  clinical psychologist from melbourne  australia which also happens to be my  hometown tony lovely to have you on the  program tell us why is cosmetic surgery  increasing during the pandemic it seems  counterintuitive  i think there are so many reasons behind  it part of it is definitely because of  lockdowns and the fact that people  couldn't access cosmetic procedures and  we know when something's forbidden and  we're not allowed it makes us want it  more  but i think as well as that people are  spending so much more time on videos  looking at themselves being able to  scrutinize their appearance and also on  social media  with not much else to do i guess while  we're stuck at home for a lot of the  time during the pandemic and that's  causing a lot more body dissatisfaction  and we're also seeing a lot more of  cosmetic surgeries available on on  social media and people publicizing it  so i think that's been a large part  it seems to be a lot more young people  which is also i would have thought  counter-intuitive and we know obviously  people do it for the aging process but  why is it mainly young people that are  seeking treatment now  yeah well i think touching on the fact  that social media has played such a big  role in this uptick in interest in  cosmetic procedures during the pandemic  we know that young people in particular  live  a lot of their lives online  and when they're spending so much time  on social media it's often promoting  this sometimes distorted or unrealistic  standard of beauty that they're then  trying to attain  and again they're they're exposed to it  they're exposed to all these cosmetic  procedures people videoing and filming  their surgeries and putting them online  and i think that's  increasing their interest in getting it  done but also we have people who had  more disposable income available to them  during the pandemic because they weren't  spending it necessarily on travel or  leisure and so now they've got this  money lying around that they can then  put towards a cosmetic surgery that they  might not have been able to afford  previously  what kind of global trends are we seeing  is this something that's happening more  in the west or you know is it really  popular in the us or australia or you  know what are the trends you're seeing  there  yeah i think we've seen it happening  around the world i've seen lots of  research and articles popping up from  across the globe everyone reporting  increased interest in cosmetic  procedures  and those are invasive and non-invasive  as well so we still see that the most  popular are the non-invasive procedures  like the botox and the dermal fillets  but during the pandemic there's also  been a massive increase in  body contouring so things like the  liposuction and tummy tuck i think  because as people's bodies might have  been changing during the pandemic  they're becoming more self-conscious and  wanting to get some of those procedures  done as well  i've also seen lots of pages on social  media on instagram for instance  debunking the before and after photos  and that kind of thing do you think  there could be a pendulum swing the  other way sort of anti-an anti-plastic  surgery wave on social media as well  if there is one it's definitely a lot  quieter unless we definitely don't see  it as much  um but i think there is you know there's  a growing wave of body positivity  influences people who are promoting body  acceptance and from time to time they  will post images like that showing the  editing of photos and the setting up of  before and after photos which i think  does help to give people a bit more of a  critical lens of what they might be  seeing on social media  people are kind of relying on these  regular beauty treatments to boost  self-esteem but do they actually help  psychologically are they beneficial do  people feel better after having uh  plastic surgery  yeah it's a great question and for the  vast majority of people they really do  so the research tells us that about 80  of people who get cosmetic procedures or  surgeries done are really happy with the  results and they report that you know  they feel better about that particular  body part that they had treated  and that it can boost their self-esteem  the thing is though that there's another  20 of people  who might be less satisfied with the  results and sometimes even experience  negative outcomes and they're usually  people like younger adults so those in  like their early 20s or even in their  teens getting cosmetic procedures done  and also people with significant mental  health issues and particular body body  dysmorphic disorder where they might not  have an accurate perception of what they  really look like  and so for those people they actually  tend to not do so well with cosmetic  procedures and they don't report the  same benefits  but for a lot of people they do find  that it boosts their self-esteem  and so just before we finish up do you  think there's a direct link between  covert 19 and the rise in plastic  surgery or is it just a coincidence and  social media  no i think that definitely video calls  video calls were something that we might  have done time to time before the  pandemic but that's been a huge part of  our lives just because of covid and i  think we're definitely seeing the zoom  boom or zoom dysmorphia rising that's  leading to that greater interest in  cosmetic procedures and also people are  struggling a lot more mentally and we  know when they're more anxious or more  depressed that that can also lead to  more body image concerns and so i think  those things are very uniquely because  of the covert 19 pandemic at the moment  that we've seen this increase in  interest in cosmetic surgery as well  all right clinical psychologist tony  picku speaking to us from melbourne  australia thanks very much  so how long could the cosmetic surgery  boom go on for here's our science  correspondent derek williams to answer  one of your questions  how long do most pandemics last  to make any predictions about how the  covet  pandemic could end it makes sense  to look at past pandemics and since they  provide data that could potentially help  us predict  how long the current one might last  experts trying to do that  tend to focus on the four officially  recognized respiratory disease pandemics  that have occurred in the last hundred  years which were all driven by an old  enemy of humanity  influenza  the most recent one was in 2009 when a  novel flu strain commonly called the  swine flu  swept the globe infecting by some  estimates up to one  in five people on the planet uh top-end  guesses say it might have caused close  to 600 000 deaths  in the 14 or so months that it raged the  swine flu pandemic was shorter  than a flu pandemic in in 1968 often  called um the hong kong flu  it was caused by a strain that killed at  least a million people worldwide  possibly many more all in all it lasted  a little less than two years  a decade before that a strain that  became widely known as the asian flu  killed a similar number of people in in  the late 1950s now what was really  interesting about that pandemic  was that it was the first where vaccines  played a key role it's thought that  their quick development and distribution  saved potentially hundreds of thousands  of lives  finally  there was the granddaddy of all modern  flu pandemics the one that devastated  the planet at the end of the first world  war  starting in around 1918.  it's viewed  as the most severe pandemic in recent  history that flu strain infected we  think around a third of the world's  population and killed at least 50  million people over the course of around  uh three years so  to sum it up  those recent pandemics lasted between  around a year and and three years but  they were of course caused by flu  viruses not coronaviruses so  so trying to draw parallels with the  current pandemic experts say  is a tenuous move at best  [Music]  and finally classical music has a long  history here in germany but the pandemic  has made it difficult to stage concerts  so one orchestra in the city of dresden  treated music lovers to a different kind  of symphony take a look  [Applause]  a concert like no other  the banks of the elba river played host  to a most unusual melody  [Music]  a steamboat symphony  [Music]  german engineering with a baroque twist  a noteworthy experience for the audience  i'm here because i think the project is  great it's extraordinary and after this  period of covet 19 which isn't over it's  finally a little glimmer of hope and  it's good for the heart and the soul  [Music]  a chance to blow off some steam  while social distancing  [Music]  well that's our show for today if you'd  like more on any of our stories you'll  find that and much more on our website  dw.com forward slash covert19 we'll be  back at the same time next week hope you  can join us then thanks for watching

